Rewritten: the two things the plugin did for a user, as a cell with no
desktop page in it. The local review: the same bounded, read-only walk over
source and configuration files, the same skip list for dependency folders
and for sensitive names (.env, credentials, private keys) whose contents
are never read, the same PHP rules (request input reaching eval(),
unserialize() or a command function; the session serializer and upload
progress advisories; display_errors), the same provider-key shape in
readable source, the same .env ignore-policy hint, the same coverage
bookkeeping that counts every entry not read under a reason, and the same
finding shape with the file and line and no source quoted back. The
offline PHP advisory assessment, with its fixed branches and its refusal of
a packaged version, and the report comparison (persisting, newly observed,
not observed again) are here too. The website assessment: one POST to the
fixed VibeLeak origin for an approved host under one scan unit, the saved
scan read back by id, the saved markdown report, and the comparison of two
saved scans -- the same endpoints, the same bounded reads, the owner key in
the Authorization header and nowhere else. The Python is Common Lisp here:
NLK:DEFINE-CONTRACT carries the tool arguments, the walk is UIOP and
CL-PPCRE, and the one network call is NLK:HTTP.

Left out: the Hermes Desktop page and everything that was only a surface
on it -- the dithered globe, the ASCII and list views, the dialogs, the
clipboard and chat handoff, the folder picker, the three.js bundle and the
DB-IP country database with its Cloudflare DNS lookup. Also left out: the
plugin host's FastAPI routes, its sqlite receipt ledger and its
installation-bound HMAC so that a saved scan is tied to one installation;
this cell keeps no state of its own, and a call it makes is the approval
the page used to ask for. The remote-asset preparation scripts, the
packaging script and the vendored maxminddb reader have no counterpart,
because a cell reads no country database.
